    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Anthropogenic stressors such as urbanization can cause physiological stress, which may impair the immune system response of wild animal populations and lead to dysfunction in wildlife communities. Animals with weakened immune systems are thus more susceptible to parasites and pathogens, which healthy animals might successfully eliminate or control. One way to measure the health of a population is to monitor parasite abundance and diversity within a population of wild animals. Yet few studies have directly documented the parasite load of wild animals in urban environments. The goals of this study were to (1) determine the parasite presence and diversity in four predator species at Jasper Ridge Biological Preserve: puma (Puma concolor), bobcat (Lynx rufus), coyote (Canis latrans), gray fox (Urocyon cinereoargenteus); (2) examine seasonal variation in numbers of infected animals and diversity of parasites; and (3) compare parasite identification methods (flotations vs DNA metabarcoding). Both morphological identification (72/113) and genetic identification (92/143) found 64% of samples tested to be positive for parasitic presence. Parasite prevalence amongst the species ranged from 40% (coyote) to 76% (bobcat) according to morphological data and from 50% (puma) to 69% (grey fox) according to genetic data. Seventeen morphologically distinct parasite species were discovered from fecal floatation which were conservatively grouped into eight separate morphotypes. Morphological data found higher parasite presence in the dry season, but there was no seasonal effect according to genetic data. Understanding the parasite load of wildlife populations will, in turn, help to protect domesticated animals as parasite spillover poses a threat to all animals in human-dominated systems.",
